{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Develop the low-overhead dense reference candidate. Replace the two DP rows and row copy with one in-place row: initialize dp[0]=1; start each row with carry=0; a blocked cell resets both dp[j] and carry to zero; otherwise update both to dp[j]+carry modulo MOD. Since both operands are residues, one conditional subtraction replaces %. Investigate faster input and row-grouped, sorted, deduplicated blocked columns, processing uninterrupted free spans without a blocked-cell lookup at every position. This permits O(n+q) storage for q distinct blockers. Compare preprocessing overhead against compact bitmap storage rather than assuming sparse storage always wins. Preserve arbitrary input order, duplicate blockers, n=1, and blocked endpoints. Deliver a complete validated candidate with measurements through the unchanged runner.\",\n  \"B\": \"Develop an exact sparse-obstacle alternative. Deduplicate blockers, handle blocked endpoints, sort remaining coordinates lexicographically, and append the destination. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2n-2; this bound is below MOD. For each point (r_i,c_i), compute w_i=C(r_i+c_i,r_i) minus the sum of w_j*C(r_i-r_j+c_i-c_j,r_i-r_j) over earlier blockers with r_j<=r_i and c_j<=c_i, modulo MOD. The destination value is the answer. Use 64-bit products and bounded modular accumulation; an unrestricted sum of products can overflow. Target O(n+q²) time and O(n+q) storage, including a genuine obstacle-free binomial path. Sparsity alone does not make q² affordable: retain a dense fallback and establish a crossover from measured costs and actual input dimensions, not test identities.\",\n  \"C\": \"Research coarse-grained parallel dense DP for the four ARM64 cores, using only facilities supported by the unchanged build. Partition the grid into tiles with top and left input boundaries and bottom and right output boundaries; process independent tiles on the same anti-diagonal concurrently. Use tile-local rolling storage and recycle boundary buffers instead of allocating an n-by-n DP matrix. Keep a persistent team of at most four workers, avoid per-cell synchronization, and preserve exact modular arithmetic and blocked-cell resets. Compare serial tiling with two- and four-worker variants over a bounded selection of tile sizes, including partial edge tiles. Measure scheduling and startup costs against the scalar candidate; retain serial execution when threading loses on small inputs. Deliver a standalone validated program, not merely an isolated kernel timing.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate only measured, correct programs from the research trees. First compare the untouched baseline and each standalone candidate using the fixed validation and timing workflow. Share parsing and obstacle preprocessing where beneficial, but avoid unconditional factorial construction, repeated sorting, or redundant representations. Consider a hybrid selecting sparse combinatorics, scalar dense DP, or tiled parallel DP from general input properties such as n and the distinct blocker count, with thresholds justified by measurements. Revalidate and remeasure each actual combination: improvements are not additive, and preprocessing or parallel overhead can erase kernel gains. Check duplicate and unordered blockers, n=1, empty grids, blocked endpoints, complete barriers, and modular wraparound without changing tests or the oracle. Keep the best standalone candidate if integration regresses; do not alter compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The supplied implementation solves Problem D; A/B/C are research roles, not the other contest problems. It performs O(n²) DP work, stores an O(n²)-bit obstacle matrix, copies a DP row after every iteration, and applies remainder at each cell. The only performance evidence is a correct baseline averaging 0.081336489 seconds with score 0.5; the unavailable diagnostic establishes no particular bottleneck. On four ARM64 CPU cores without a GPU, pursue three complementary hypotheses: lower dense-loop overhead, replace grid traversal with combinatorics when blockers are sufficiently few, and expose coarse wavefront parallelism for larger dense workloads. The short observed runtime makes preprocessing and synchronization costs important. No input-size distribution or demonstrated optimization gains are available, so all selections require actual measurements and must remain correct for every valid input.\"\n}"}}
